\chapter{The weft Language}
\label{ch:language}

The whole of weft is a handful of commands that all begin with the escape
character \verb|@@|. Everything else in a \verb|.weft| file --- \LaTeX\ or
Markdown prose --- is copied through untouched. This chapter is the
complete reference: defining files and fragments, the scraps that hold
code, parameters, the minor utility commands, and section scoping.

\section{Output files and fragments}

Two commands produce all of weft's structure. \verb|@@o| names an
\emph{output file} --- a tangle target written to disk. \verb|@@d| names a
\emph{fragment} --- a reusable, named piece of code that is spliced into
files (or other fragments) by reference.

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|@@o| \textit{file flags scrap} & Output a file. The name ends at whitespace. \\
\verb|@@d| \textit{name scrap}       & Define a fragment. The name ends at a return or a scrap. \\
\verb|@@q| \textit{name scrap}       & Define a \emph{quoted} fragment (see below). \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

A file or a fragment may be defined in \emph{several pieces}: repeat the
\verb|@@o| or \verb|@@d| with the same name and weft concatenates the
parts, in source order, into one file or one fragment. This is what lets
you grow a definition across the narrative --- add to a header here,
extend a function there --- and is shown in the woven output by the
\hbox{$+\!\equiv$} continuation marker.

The capital forms \verb|@@O|, \verb|@@D|, and \verb|@@Q| behave exactly
like their lowercase counterparts but force a page break before the
definition when weaving, which is handy for keeping a large scrap
together on one page.

\subsection{Quoted fragments}

A \emph{quoted} fragment (\verb|@@q|) is defined but \emph{not expanded}
when tangling: instead of splicing its body, weft writes it out as a
fragment \emph{use}, so the output file can itself be valid \verb|.weft|
source. This lets you generate weft files that undergo further processing
before their fragments are expanded, while still describing and
documenting them in one place. Inside a quoted fragment, \verb|@@f| (the
file name) is quoted too, so it refers to the finally produced file, not
an intermediate one. You may mix \verb|@@q| and \verb|@@d| definitions of
the same name; each part is emitted in order, quoted or expanded as
declared.

\section{Scraps and what lives inside them}

A \emph{scrap} is the body of code (or text) attached to a definition.
Its delimiters are chosen so you can hold any content exactly, including
every blank, tab, and carriage return:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|@@{| \textit{code} \verb|@@}| & Verbatim code. The usual form; typeset with \texttt{listings}. \\
\verb|@@[| \textit{text} \verb|@@]| & Paragraph mode: a piece of typeset \TeX. \\
\verb|@@(| \textit{math} \verb|@@)| & Math mode: a formula, typeset nicely. \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

Any amount of whitespace (including newlines) may sit between a name and
the start of its scrap. A scrap may also appear in the running prose,
where it is typeset like a definition but contributes no code to any
output file --- useful for talking about fragments in the text.

\subsection{Inside a scrap}

Within a scrap body, weft recognises a few sequences:

\begin{description}
\item[\tt @@<\textit{name}@@>] Expand the named fragment inline (recursion
is an error). When the reference appears \emph{mid-line} --- after at
least one visible character --- section markers, \verb|#line| directives,
and name comments are suppressed and the fragment is spliced seamlessly;
at the start of a line they are emitted normally
(Chapter~\ref{ch:languages}).
\item[\tt @@1 \ldots @@9] Substitute the $n$th argument of the enclosing
fragment (empty if not supplied). See \S\ref{sec:params}.
\item[\tt @@f] The current output file's name.
\item[\tt @@t] The current fragment's title, with parameters replaced by
their actual arguments.
\item[\tt @@x\textit{label}@@x] Mark a place inside the scrap and bind it
to \textit{label} (any text without an \verb|@@|). Expands to the scrap's
reference number, so prose elsewhere can point at a spot within a scrap.
\item[\tt @@\#] At the start of a line, suppress that line's indentation
--- write \verb|@@#| before a \verb|#ifdef| so it sits flush left instead
of following the surrounding indentation.
\item[\tt @@s] Suppress the indentation of the \emph{next} fragment
expansion.
\item[\tt @@\%] Comment out the rest of the line, exactly like \verb|%| in
ordinary \LaTeX. Handy for disabling a line of code during testing
without disturbing real comments.
\item[{\tt @@| \textit{ids} @@\}}] At the very end of a scrap, list
identifiers to record in the \verb|@@u| index (\S\ref{sec:minor}).
\end{description}

\subsection{Abbreviating long names}

A fragment name may be \emph{abbreviated} at a definition or a use: type a
unique prefix followed by \verb|...|. So

\begin{quote}
\verb|@@d Check for terminating at-sequence and return name if found|
\end{quote}

\noindent can later be written

\begin{quote}
\verb|@@d Check for terminating...|
\end{quote}

\noindent weft keeps the longest form it sees, and the abbreviation may
even appear before the full name. Blanks and tabs inside a name are
insignificant --- each run collapses to a single space.

\subsection{Tabs and indentation}

When a scrap is written out, tabs are expanded to spaces by default (tab
stops every eight columns), and an expanded fragment is indented to match
the indentation of its \hbox{\tt @@<\ldots@@>} reference. Take care with
layout-sensitive languages (Fortran, Python); the per-file flags below
let you turn either behaviour off.

\subsection{Per-file flags}
\label{sec:fileflags}

An \verb|@@o| directive may carry flags that tune how \emph{that one}
file is tangled --- introducing a little language-specific behaviour
without affecting the rest of the document:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|-d| & Emit \verb|#line| directives (C/C++/Fortran source debugging). \\
\verb|-i| & Do not indent expanded fragments (useful for Fortran). \\
\verb|-t| & Do not expand tabs (useful for \texttt{make}files). \\
\verb|-c|\textit{x} & Comment each fragment in the code: \verb|c|=\verb|/* */|, \verb|+|=\verb|//|, \verb|p|=\verb|#|. \\
\verb|-s| & Suppress section markers and \verb|#line| for this file. \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent In most cases the automatic language detection of
Chapter~\ref{ch:languages} is more convenient than these flags, but they
remain for explicit control --- for example, \verb|-s| keeps bootstrap or
generated files free of tangle annotations.

\section{Fragment names with parameters}
\label{sec:params}

Fragment names can carry \emph{parameters}: placeholder values written
into the name with the \verb|@@'value@@'| syntax. Inside the body,
\verb|@@1| \ldots \verb|@@9| refer to them in order of appearance. A
reusable validation fragment, for instance:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
@@d Validate field @@'name@@' type @@'string@@'
@@{if (!data.@@1) throw new Error("Missing @@1");
if (typeof data.@@1 !== '@@2')
  throw new TypeError("@@1 must be @@2");
@@}
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent Each use supplies its own arguments:

\begin{quote}
\begin{verbatim}
@@<Validate field @@'email@@' type @@'string@@'@@>
@@<Validate field @@'age@@'   type @@'number@@'@@>
\end{verbatim}
\end{quote}

\noindent so the first expands to \verb|if (!data.email) ...|. Three
things to note: the values in the \emph{definition} act as default
arguments and as documentation (they make the name readable); a
\emph{use} may substitute different values; and if a name is abbreviated
with \verb|...|, any missing arguments fall back to the definition's
defaults.

At each argument position of a use, four forms are accepted:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|@@'string@@'|          & A literal string (only \verb|@@@@| is interpreted). Most common. \\
\verb|@@<|\textit{name}\verb|@@>| & A fragment; its expansion becomes the argument. \\
\verb|@@{|\textit{text}\verb|@@}| & An anonymous scrap (inherits the caller's arguments). \\
\verb|@@1| \ldots \verb|@@9| & Forward an argument from the calling fragment. \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\section{Minor commands}
\label{sec:minor}

These low-level utilities may appear anywhere in a web:

\begin{description}
\item[\tt @@@@] Copy a single \verb|@@| to the output. (Every literal
at-sign in your desired output is doubled in the source.)
\item[\tt @@\_\textit{text}@@\_] Set the enclosed text bold --- for
keywords in a scrap.
\item[\tt @@i \textit{file}] Include another file (nested up to 10 deep).
The name is taken literally (no extension appended) and ends at a
newline. The current directory is searched, plus any directories given
with \verb|-I|, in order.
\item[\tt @@r\textit{x}] Change the escape character from \verb|@@| to
\textit{x}. Must precede any scrap definitions.
\item[\tt @@v] The version string --- the value set by \verb|-V|, or, by
default, weft's own version. Use it to stamp generated files.
\item[\tt @@l \textit{tag}] Set the language for the next \verb|@@o| or
\verb|@@d| (Chapter~\ref{ch:languages}). Place it on the line before the
directive.
\item[\tt @@L \textit{name style} {\rm[}+d{\rm]}] Define a new language
mapping. Must appear in the preamble, before any directive that uses it.
\item[\tt @@W \textit{format}] Declare this file's weave format,
\verb|tex| or \verb|md|; used by \verb|-w| when no format is given on the
command line. Put it in the preamble.
\item[\tt @@c] Outside a scrap, define a block comment (all text up to the
next command); inside a scrap, expand the last block comment at the
current indentation, wrapping lines past 60 columns.
\end{description}

\subsection{Indices}

Three commands generate back-of-document indices, usually each in its own
section:

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|@@f| & Index of output file names. \\
\verb|@@m| & Index of fragment names. \\
\verb|@@u| & Index of user-specified identifiers. \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

\noindent The identifier index is opt-in: you \emph{mark} each identifier
at the end of a scrap with \hbox{\tt @@| FOO BAR @@\}}. All \emph{uses} of
those identifiers inside scraps are then found automatically. Any string
without whitespace or \verb|@@| works, so even \verb|<<=| can be indexed;
an identifier may be marked in more than one scrap. In the index each
identifier lists the scraps that use and define it, with defining scraps
underlined. (An identifier need not literally appear in the scrap that
declares it --- it only has to be in that scrap's closing list.)

\section{Sectioning: scoped names}

In a large web the indices and name spaces grow unwieldy, and it becomes
easy to reuse a fragment name by accident. \emph{Sections} give each part
of the document its own scope: a fragment named in one section is
unrelated to a same-named fragment in another, and identifiers do not
leak between them. (Scraps in successive sections can still target the
same output file.)

\begin{quote}
\begin{tabular}{@@{}ll@@{}}
\verb|@@s| & Start a new local section. \\
\verb|@@S| & Close the current section (following text is the base section). \\
\verb|@@d+| \textit{name} & Define a \emph{global} fragment, visible in all sections. \\
\verb|@@D+| / \verb|@@q+| / \verb|@@Q+| & The page-break / quoted variants. \\
\verb|@@m+| / \verb|@@u+| & Index the global fragments / identifiers. \\
\end{tabular}
\end{quote}

There are two kinds of section: the \emph{base} section, where everything
normally lives, and \emph{local} sections introduced by \verb|@@s| and
ended by \verb|@@S| (or the next \verb|@@s|). Base-section fragments are
not global --- they are reachable only within the base section, but from
anywhere in it regardless of intervening local sections. When you really
do need to cross a boundary, within a scrap:

\begin{description}
\item[\tt @@<+\textit{name}@@>] Expand the \emph{global} fragment of that
name rather than a local one.
\item[\tt @@+] Like \hbox{\tt @@|} but \emph{exports} the identifiers to
the global realm (not referenced from any single scrap).
\item[\tt @@-] Like \hbox{\tt @@|} but \emph{imports} global identifiers
into the local realm; uses within the section point here, and the
cross-references show where the globals are defined.
\end{description}

\noindent The \verb|+| is part of the command, not the name: to define a
fragment whose name begins with \verb|+|, leave a space after the
command.
